            "value": [
              {
                "dsDescriptionValue": {
                  "typeName": "dsDescriptionValue",
                  "multiple": false,
                  "typeClass": "primitive",
                  "value": "Introduction: A widely used non-operative treatment for adolescent idiopathic scoliosis is\nbracing. Yet, traditional handmade rigid thoracolumbar braces pose challenges related to\nvariability, practicality, and compliance. Innovative 3D-printed braces potentially overcome some of these limitations but clear criteria for the trajectory from brace development to successful clinical implementation are currently lacking. This study will propose mechanical tests and assess the feasibility for subsequent implementation into clinical practice of a novel 3D-printed brace for treatment of adolescent idiopathic scoliosis.\n\nMethods and analysis: This prospective, cross-sectional feasibility study consists of two\nparts: (1) (bio-)mechanical evaluation and (2) evaluation in a clinical setting of a 3D-printed\nbrace in 20 patients with adolescent idiopathic scoliosis. The mechanical evaluation will\nassess the structural properties of the 3D-printed scoliosis brace. Clinical evaluation will\ndetermine safety, comfort, limited efficacy of in-brace correction and manufacturing costs for the 3D-printed scoliosis brace as compared to a conventional brace used in standard care.\n\nEthics and dissemination: Ethical approval is pending review and will be obtained from\nthe Medical Ethical Testing Committee of MUMC+ before the study commences. Results will be published in a peer-reviewed journal and additionally disseminated through scientific conferences and social media aiming to reach a broad audience including patients."
                },
                "dsDescriptionDate": {
                  "typeName": "dsDescriptionDate",
                  "multiple": false,
                  "typeClass": "primitive",
